talk about databases or file

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:16.630
systems.

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:22.210
But because Kubernetes was
built with scalability in place,

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:27.790
and because GKE is the best
hosted version of Kubernetes,

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.040
you should have
super-high expectations

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.950
about what's happening.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:37.120
All right, so before we
go there, a quick recap.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.310
What's happening at Kubernetes?

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.920
Kubernetes is very simple.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.391
In Kubernetes, you
have a podspec.

00:03:43.391 --> 00:03:44.890
In the podspec,
you're saying, well,

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:47.320
here's my Docker container.

00:03:47.320 --> 00:03:50.650
I want this many replicas of it.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:55.180
And, well, this is the amount
of resources I want to use.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:59.710
Once you have it, you give
the config file to Kubernetes

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:00.460
master.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:06.280
You put the container
image to the repository.

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.180
And then Kubernetes master
is consulting its scheduler

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:12.690
and saying, hello,
dear scheduler.

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.640
Could you please tell me
where is the best place

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.890
I can deploy this very pod.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:22.360
So scheduler is looking at
the lower layer on the picture

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:26.200
where all of the nodes are
located and saying, here.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:31.630
And then Kubernetes downloads
the pod from the repository

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:35.630
and deploys it on the cluster.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:39.070
And then it's maintaining
it through its life.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:41.140
So whatever happens
with the pod,

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.300
it will get restarted
by Kubernetes

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:45.460
as that is watching it.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:48.050
It's always going
to be there for you.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:52.740
Now, as you saw in
this picture, we

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.100
are, in fact, talking
about more than one layer.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.690
You have the
infrastructure layer

00:04:57.690 --> 00:05:01.320
on the bottom where
your virtual machines

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.800
or your physical machines are.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.110
We call them nodes
in Kubernetes.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.704
And then you have
the work load layer.

00:05:08.704 --> 00:05:09.870
And this is the fancy layer.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:13.560
This is the abstraction layer
where everything is beautiful

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:14.910
and you just deploy your pods.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:20.220
And we expect you
to just have fun

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:23.520
deploying your pods without
worrying about the lower part.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.500
So because of that, and
because of these two layers,

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:33.030
you in fact have also more than
one dimension of autoscaling.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:36.510
So in the upper layer, well,
you start from horizontal pod

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:37.980
autoscaling.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.330
You have the pods.

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:45.420
And in case of the workloads
that you can load balance

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:51.570
in any way, like your mobile
front end or your website,

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:54.380
well, if there is more
traffic, you need more pods.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.420
If there is less traffic,
you should need less pods.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.460
And autoscaler does it for you.

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:02.210
And this works well.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.540
But not always
because in some cases

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.750
you actually need to
change the pod requests.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:10.720
You may want to say, I want
double the amount of processor

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:12.707
or triple the amount of memory.

00:06:12.707 --> 00:06:14.290
And that's the
vertical scaling of it.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:16.840
That's a totally
different image.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:20.000
And that was the workload
layer, the fancy layer.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.440
And now realistically, then,
you have this other layer.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:30.190
And it's different between
the on premises and the cloud.

00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:33.670
On premises, well, you pretty
much have what you have.

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:36.340
So that's your sunk cost.

00:06:36.340 --> 00:06:38.560
And the best you can
do is to use them all.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.730
Ideally, just use 100 percent
CPU utilization on them

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:44.050
and be done.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:47.890
Nobody ever achieved that,
but it's like, use them.

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:50.310
In the cloud, you
don't have to do that.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:53.350
In the cloud, in fact,
you have the ability

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:58.300
to request exactly what you
need and then use it only

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:59.620
when you need it.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.980
And this goes a long way.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:04.900
Like, you may request
specific types

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:10.520
of machines that are
having the amount of memory

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:13.780
and the number, of course, that
exactly correspond to what you

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:15.620
want to deploy on top of it.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:17.410
You may want to
request something

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:22.090
that is even more sophisticated
because it has a graphics

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:22.960
accelerator.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.000
All of that is
possible in the cloud.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.150
Not so much, and without any
flexibility, on premises.

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:32.890
So now, well, on the
infrastructure layer

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:38.200
in the cloud, you also
have abilities to scale.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.680
This goes roughly the same way.

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:44.560
Meaning, well, if there
are more workloads deployed

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.840
from the workload
layer, then you're

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:50.980
going to need more nodes
in the infrastructure layer

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:52.400
to handle it.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.200
So the idea here is to
reactively, very quickly,

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.540
get you the appropriate
type of a node

00:07:58.540 --> 00:08:02.036
and remove them as soon
as they're not needed.

00:08:02.036 --> 00:08:03.410
So this is something
that we call

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:06.040
a cluster autoscaler,
so autoscaler number

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.140
three in this story.

00:08:08.140 --> 00:08:09.640
And then there is
one more piece.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:11.660
And this one more
piece, I'm very proud.

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:13.990
It's an alpha, and it's
coming to beta hopefully

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:14.770
later this summer.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.440
It's called node
auto-provisioning.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.600
And it's getting a
different type of nodes

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:20.890
depending on what you need.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:23.560
And you don't need to
really worry about it.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.260
So we are going to
talk piece by piece

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:28.000
about all of these
four dimensions,

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.560
giving you great demos.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.940
My friend is resting
here in the corner,

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:36.640
but he'll be up to
speed in a second.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:37.299
All right.

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:42.700
So here in the story, we have
four dimensions of autoscaling.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.220
HBA, elsewhere call it
Horizontal Pod Autoscaler,

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.280
more pods based on the
utilization matrix.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.910
Vertical Pod Autoscaler,
different pod sizes,

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:53.690
right amount.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:58.820
Cluster autoscaler, more
nodes based on pending pods

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:00.020
in the scheduler.

00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:01.960
And then node
auto-provisioning, all together

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:06.020
they are different node pools
and the overall scheduling

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:06.736
optimizer.

00:09:06.736 --> 00:09:08.360
This is what we're
going to talk about.

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.240
So let's talk about horizontal
pod autoscaling in practice.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.875
So this is an example of a
workload that is load balanced.

00:09:15.875 --> 00:09:19.460
So imagine we have
four pods on the left.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:23.480
And what you see on the right
is their CPU utilization

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:26.720
expressed in terms of
how much the pod is using

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:30.200
versus what was requested.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:34.310
So as you can see, these
pods are running hot.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:37.910
But if you manage
to add one more pod,

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:39.770
then the load balancer
that you're using

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:43.310
can spread the workload
across more pods.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:48.110
And through this, the average
utilization will be lower.

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:50.900
And the same story will happen
if you go the other direction.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:53.910
Imagine you were at
the stage like this.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:58.850
All of the pods were running
below 50% of CPU utilization.

00:09:58.850 --> 00:10:05.690
Now we must say, well, we are
heating up the data center.

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:10.150
50% of our cores are
basically doing nothing.

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:16.296
So in the ideal case, we would
just remove one of these pods

00:10:16.296 --> 00:10:20.430
and get the load denser
on the other four.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:27.080
So pretty much this
all comes to a target.

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:29.300
You set a target
and you say, I want

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:34.150
my workload to run, on average,
across all pods I deployed,

00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:38.440
around something like this,
which is maybe like 60%, say.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:44.250
And then autoscaler is getting
the workload to that state.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:46.050
It's adding more
pods when needed.

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:47.930
It's removing them
where not needed,

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:52.310
observing all of the loads
of your cluster as they are.

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:55.870
So that was the situation where
you have the state proportional

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:57.392
to the number of requests.

00:10:57.392 --> 00:10:58.600
But it's not always the case.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:01.220
And that's why we have something
that we call custom metrics.

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:03.150
In the situation
of custom metrics,

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:07.220
you may extract any
metrics from your system.

00:11:07.220 --> 00:11:09.770
And this can be number
of requests per seconds.

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:11.810
This can be latencies
of your system.

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:17.600
This can finally be
something like backlog

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:21.350
of tasks in the task
queue, which we have here.

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:25.130
So imagine there is
a very simple queue.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:27.920
We use Pub/Sub queue
because we are Google.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.859
But you may use any other queue.

00:11:29.859 --> 00:11:30.650
It can be RabbitMQ.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.530
It can be whatever, ZooKeeper.

00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:39.210
And then you have producers
that deliver a piece of data

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:41.870
to be processed and
register this fact

00:11:41.870 --> 00:11:44.090
in the queue as a message.

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:47.390
And then you have workers
that pick up this message

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:48.700
and start to process it.

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:51.350
And then at the end,
you have autoscaler.

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.780
And what the autoscaler
is doing is making sure

00:11:53.780 --> 00:11:57.230
that there is enough workers
to get this data processed

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:58.190
in a timely manner.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:11:59.200
That's how it works.

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:02.360
Super powerful.

00:12:02.360 --> 00:12:05.300
Tens and tens of customers
that we talked to that

00:12:05.300 --> 00:12:07.220
just need a system
like that in order

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:13.490
to process data workloads
in a timely manner

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:15.380
in a very controlled way.

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:18.075
So let's get started.

00:12:18.075 --> 00:12:21.922
I'm going to invite Ignacy
Kowalczyk to stage to walk you

00:12:21.922 --> 00:12:22.880
through the first part.

00:12:31.502 --> 00:12:34.642
So you can switch to the other--

00:12:34.642 --> 00:12:35.850
IGNACY KOWALCZYK: --everyone.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.830
And thank you, Jerzy,
for a great introduction

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:39.870
to autoscaling in GKE.

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.580
So it's going to be
my pleasure today

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:44.220
to demonstrate how all these
dimensions of autoscaling

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:46.700
actually work in GKE.

00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:49.530
I'm going to start with, in
the first part of my demo,

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.630
with setting up
the horizontal pod

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:55.260
autoscaler for the pool of
workers that will be consuming

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:57.102
tasks from a message queue.

00:12:57.102 --> 00:12:58.810
These are going to be
three simple steps.

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:03.531
First of all, we're going to set
up the workers, the task queue.

00:13:03.531 --> 00:13:06.520
The second step
will be configuring

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:09.060
the autoscaler that's
based on the size of the,

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:11.750
or the number of
elements in the queue.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.530
We'll autoscale the workers.

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:17.130
And finally, we'll add
some tasks into the queue,

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:20.100
just to see the system rolling.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:23.124
All right, but
before I get started,

00:13:23.124 --> 00:13:26.310
let me show you the
environment we are using here,

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:29.580
so basically what
cluster I'm using.

00:13:42.410 --> 00:13:46.055
So as you can see, there's a
quite funny name of a cluster.

00:13:46.055 --> 00:13:47.680
But it's actually a
development cluster

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:50.260
so that I can show you some
of the cool features of VPN

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:53.130
Cluster Autoscaler that
are not launched yet.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:53.630
All right.

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:55.790
Let's take a little bit
closer look at the cluster.

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:57.790
So I'm going to check
what nodes do I have here.

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:04.790
So my cluster is actually
running five nodes,

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:08.650
each of them being
n1-standard-1 VMs in GCE.

00:14:08.650 --> 00:14:10.000
OK, so I've got my cluster.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:11.700
So the next thing
is the workload.

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:15.470
I actually set up my worker
pool before this presentation

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:18.590
has started, as well, through
a deployment controller.

00:14:23.672 --> 00:14:25.880
So deployment is called worker.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:32.520
Now, let's take a look at the
definition of this deployment.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.670
So the important thing is
we've got two replicas.

00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:36.890
So we start with
two worker tasks.

00:14:36.890 --> 00:14:39.350
All these talks will have
the label [? app:worker ?]

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:40.460
assigned.

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:44.204
And you might also notice
that because finding

00:14:44.204 --> 00:14:45.620
the right podspec
is so difficult,

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:49.880
I just didn't set any
requests for my memory.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:55.930
So going back to the
console, so next thing,

00:14:55.930 --> 00:14:59.049
once we have the workers, let's
check out our message queue.

00:14:59.049 --> 00:15:01.090
So as Jerzy mentioned,
we're using cloud Pub/Sub.

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:09.990
So we've got one
topic defined which

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:14.190
the producers will be using to
publish tasks into the message

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:16.140
queue.

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:23.370
And we have one
subscription that

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:25.680
is called a worker's
subscription

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:28.080
that's letting the
consumers consume messages

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:31.140
from the worker task queue.

00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:33.930
Our setup is such
that each worker task

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:37.230
is consuming messages from
a single subscription.

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:39.600
So therefore, what you're
modeling is effectively a task

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:41.160
queue where each
task in the queue

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:44.580
will be delivered to
exactly one worker.

00:15:44.580 --> 00:15:47.340
All right, so now the next step,
once we have worker, queue,

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:48.910
now horizontal pod autoscaler.

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:51.797
So let me create
horizontal pod autoscaler.

00:16:03.725 --> 00:16:05.664
Am I in the right directory?

00:16:05.664 --> 00:16:06.164
Yes.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:14.160
All right.

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:16.110
So let's take a look
again at the definition

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:18.396
of this horizontal
pod autoscaler.

00:16:18.396 --> 00:16:20.220
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:22.171
JERZY FORYCIARZ:
It hasn't started.

00:16:22.171 --> 00:16:25.016
IGNACY KOWALCZYK: Ah, sorry.

00:16:25.016 --> 00:16:28.130
Yeah, this is a
live demo, you see?

00:16:28.130 --> 00:16:30.650
I didn't pray to the demo
gods that well, possibly.

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:32.441
Thank you.

00:16:32.441 --> 00:16:33.940
All right, so back
to the definition

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:35.314
of our horizontal
pod autoscaler.

00:16:35.314 --> 00:16:37.880
So the interesting
bit here is that we

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:41.540
are using number of pending
messages in our queue

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:43.346
as a scaling metric.

00:16:43.346 --> 00:16:44.470
This is an external metric.

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:47.260
This is supposed in the
V2 version, of V2 API

00:16:47.260 --> 00:16:49.160
of horizontal pod autoscaler.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:54.200
Now, the target average
value, what does it mean?

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:58.030
That means that we want
to have, on average,

00:16:58.030 --> 00:17:02.020
25 messages in our
queue per every pod

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:04.140
replica in our deployment.

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:08.839
So roughly that means we
want one worker processing

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:11.930
the tasks per every
25 tasks in the queue.

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:14.390
And as you can see, we
are also, obviously,

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:17.089
pointing our horizontal pod
autoscaler at the worker

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:19.770
deployment down here.

00:17:19.770 --> 00:17:24.470
All right, so now let's
take a closer look

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:30.230
at this horizontal pod
autoscaler using kubectl.

00:17:30.230 --> 00:17:32.000
So the horizontal
pod autoscaler has

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:33.290
been successfully recreated.

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:35.720
The name of the HPA
is queue scaler.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:37.350
Now the interesting
column that I

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:41.210
want to drag your attention
to is the targets column.

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:46.800
So this is where, actually, the
target metric is represented.

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:49.340
So the value zero
means that currently we

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:52.550
have zero messages in the
queue for every worker

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:59.520
in our deployment while
the target value is 25.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.450
The reason why horizontal pod
autoscaler is not scaling down

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:04.220
our deployment is
that, actually, we

00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:06.859
have set up the minimum and
maximum number of pod replicas

00:18:06.859 --> 00:18:07.650
for our deployment.

00:18:07.650 --> 00:18:09.530
And the minimum number
is two, so we're

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:14.550
kind of staying with
two replicas here.

00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:17.060
All right, with that we are
moving to the third step, which

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:19.910
is, let's add some
messages into the queue

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:22.410
to see things rolling.

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:24.800
So I have a little script
that will be adding tasks.

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.960
And I'm going to show you
how this script actually

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:29.670
looks like.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:32.000
Again, a super simple thing.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:36.620
So this basically generates 100
messages into our worker tasks

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:43.040
topic, and doing so with
[INAUDIBLE] and messages

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:44.360
in each.

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:47.330
So this seems to be
pretty simple mechanism.

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:51.740
But actually the cool thing is
that is a very powerful design

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:52.820
pattern.

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:56.240
In fact, last year
on the Next 2017

00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:57.830
we showcased
together with Airbus

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:01.565
their solution where they were
processing satellite images

00:19:01.565 --> 00:19:02.690
exactly with this approach.

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:04.231
So what they've been
doing is they've

00:19:04.231 --> 00:19:09.560
been publishing the images to
be transcoded into the message

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:10.220
queue.

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:14.720
And then they were running
a horizontally scaled pool

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:17.764
of workers that were
processing these tasks.

00:19:17.764 --> 00:19:19.430
And obviously,
horizontal pod autoscaler

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:21.110
was managed to find
a good trade-off

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:24.300
between the performance of
the system and the cost.

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:27.800
So in the meantime, I hope
my message is good at it.

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:28.820
OK.

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:33.290
And then let's see what
horizontal pod autoscaler has

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:34.686
to say about it now.

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:43.460
All right, so what we see
is that the metric is still

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:47.110
zero, which means our horizontal
pod autoscaler did not

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:53.770
