00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:17.420
All right, so back to
the slides, please.

00:22:17.420 --> 00:22:25.450
So yes, besides demo gods,
nothing else can go wrong, huh?

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:26.640
Well, not quite.

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:30.220
We've been actually
surveying you for a reason.

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:33.390
Well, realistically,
how many of you

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:38.760
run very detailed
performance tests every time

00:22:38.760 --> 00:22:42.780
you deploy a new deployment?

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:44.740
OK, 1, 2, 3.

00:22:44.740 --> 00:22:45.920
OK.

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:51.210
And deployments typically
do per day in Kubernetes?

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:56.390
I'm assuming a lot.

00:22:56.390 --> 00:23:00.950
And in that case, this
may get problematic if all

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:04.820
of the sudden this situation
changes in your pod,

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:07.000
and you do not correct
it in the podspec.

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:10.330
So imagine in this situation--
we're obviously setting up

00:23:10.330 --> 00:23:13.760
for that to demonstrate--
imagine in this situation we

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:17.160
started to run out of memory
because the very last build

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:18.620
added just one more feature.

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:20.731
And this one more feature
is using far more memory

00:23:20.731 --> 00:23:21.230
than before.

00:23:21.230 --> 00:23:22.820
Nothing is wrong with it.

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:29.030
It's just a mismatch between
the kind of virtual space,

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:30.930
which is your
container, which was

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:35.660
requested at this certain
size, and what you really need.

00:23:35.660 --> 00:23:39.890
So in that situation,
you go out of memory.

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:42.590
And if you go out of memory,
what's going to happen?

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:47.150
Your job is going to get
evicted by Kubernetes.

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:49.370
And then scheduler will see it.

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:51.800
And because I promise that
Kubernetes will always

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:54.780
take care of your pods,
they'll get recreated.

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:57.810
And after a while, they'll
get evicted and so on.

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:00.060
Well, there is
another possibility.

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:01.880
There is, in fact,
a race condition

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:04.690
because Unix is also very smart.

00:24:04.690 --> 00:24:10.860
So may, in fact, get your
application reset by Linux

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:12.020
before it gets evicted.

00:24:12.020 --> 00:24:14.030
But in either case,
you're going to get

00:24:14.030 --> 00:24:17.810
in this vicious cycle of not
having your workload running.

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:23.010
So with that in mind, we
built vertical pod autoscaler.

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:26.300
So vertical pod autoscaler
is a piece of beauty.

00:24:26.300 --> 00:24:28.490
It does roughly this.

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:32.960
It looks at your
workload, decides here,

00:24:32.960 --> 00:24:36.520
whatever we have in the
95% and the remaining five,

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:37.640
this was your pod.

00:24:37.640 --> 00:24:41.170
Let's say this was your pod
expressed in CPU utilization.

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:44.800
And now vertical pod autoscaler
is saying, ha, ha, ha.

00:24:44.800 --> 00:24:46.520
Just request a bigger
machine for that.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:47.720
So you get a bigger machine.

00:24:47.720 --> 00:24:49.345
And all of a sudden,
you're not running

00:24:49.345 --> 00:24:51.380
90% of the utilization
of that space.

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:52.600
You're running 70.

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:54.420
The [? world ?] is saved.

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:56.090
So now how do we do this?

00:24:56.090 --> 00:25:00.530
We do it in a very simple
way, through three steps.

00:25:00.530 --> 00:25:03.680
First one is vertical
pod autoscaler

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:06.800
is watching the utilization.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:09.080
Does it do it for
every pod you have?

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:09.590
No.

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:12.470
You actually have to turn it
on and you have to tell us--

00:25:12.470 --> 00:25:15.650
or, well, tell vertical
pod autoscaler--

00:25:15.650 --> 00:25:17.300
what is the label.

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:19.010
What pods do you want to watch?

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:23.070
Once you do this,
vertical pod autoscaler

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:25.520
consulting the metrics
server and it's

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:27.540
calculating the recommendation.

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:30.050
And at minimum, it's just
providing the recommendation.

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:32.950
And then also, you can
enable automatic update mode,

00:25:32.950 --> 00:25:34.700
and I'll talk to you
about it in a second.

00:25:34.700 --> 00:25:36.740
You can, in fact, actuate.

00:25:36.740 --> 00:25:39.059
But at minimum, this
[INAUDIBLE] podspec?

00:25:39.059 --> 00:25:41.600
Well, assume for a while that
you actually pulled the request

00:25:41.600 --> 00:25:43.720
for memory on CPU.

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:48.050
Well, and it was
wrong for some reason.

00:25:48.050 --> 00:25:50.090
So what vertical pod
autoscaler is doing

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:51.700
is consulting metric server.

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:54.610
It looks at the piece on the
left, which is your spec.

00:25:54.610 --> 00:25:59.000
And on the right, it's
outputting a very simple

00:25:59.000 --> 00:25:59.632
recommendation.

00:25:59.632 --> 00:26:01.340
And this recommendation
is in the middle.

00:26:01.340 --> 00:26:02.380
It says target.

00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:05.840
And it says, well,
CPU, two cores,

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:08.080
memory has certain amounts.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:10.280
And then it's also
providing a range.

00:26:10.280 --> 00:26:14.120
And the range is for the updater
to either take action or not

00:26:14.120 --> 00:26:14.630
take action.

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:16.130
If you're not
outside of the range,

00:26:16.130 --> 00:26:18.320
there is probably no
reason for you to update,

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:20.940
even if the target is
somewhere in between.

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:22.580
So that's how it works.

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:24.830
So now we have two
modes of update,

00:26:24.830 --> 00:26:26.720
if you choose to use it.

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:28.850
One is only
happening at the time

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:31.550
of creating of the new pod.

00:26:31.550 --> 00:26:35.020
So whenever a new
pod gets created,

00:26:35.020 --> 00:26:37.920
vertical pod autoscaler is
reading what's in the podspec.

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:40.350
And it's also comparing it
with the recommendation.

00:26:40.350 --> 00:26:42.320
And if that's wrong
or if there is

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:46.430
a mismatch outside
of the safety wrench,

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:49.200
then it applies correction.

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:52.250
So in fact, the pod that's
going to get deployed

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:54.570
has a correct request.

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:56.640
It doesn't mean that the
podspec was corrected.

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:59.380
It's just, well,
vertical pod autoscaler

00:26:59.380 --> 00:27:01.980
knows better and does it right.

00:27:01.980 --> 00:27:05.702
And then there is a second
mode that we call full auto,

00:27:05.702 --> 00:27:07.410
that we're, by the
way, going to use here

00:27:07.410 --> 00:27:11.100
in a second, that does it,
also, for all running pods.

00:27:11.100 --> 00:27:16.090
In fact, it's observing
the pod disruption budget.

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:22.140
It is taking pods one by one,
draining them, and recreating

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:23.850
or forcing to get
recreated that so

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:26.340
that they can just get adjusted.

00:27:26.340 --> 00:27:29.160
So that's it.

00:27:29.160 --> 00:27:32.520
And you may ask, I probably
already spoiled it,

00:27:32.520 --> 00:27:36.090
but you may ask, will
they play together?

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:40.020
So the answer is yes.

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:43.890
And the answer is
also, well, however,

00:27:43.890 --> 00:27:49.050
don't try it with the
horizontal pod autoscaling

00:27:49.050 --> 00:27:51.120
running on CPU
utilization because this

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:52.830
is the same metrics.

00:27:52.830 --> 00:27:54.150
It's not working quite yet.

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:57.300
So on everything else,
we promised it's working.

00:27:57.300 --> 00:28:00.590
And with this, back to
Ignacy to demonstrate.

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:03.450
IGNACY KOWALCZYK:
Thank you, Jerzy.

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:06.550
All right, so in this
part of the demo,

00:28:06.550 --> 00:28:08.910
I'm going to use
vertical pod autoscaler.

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:11.140
And as you remember,
I was quite naive.

00:28:11.140 --> 00:28:13.740
I didn't set any memory
requests for my application.

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:15.570
And this makes me a
little bit anxious.

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:19.140
You know, I deployed it,
and I kind of hope it runs.

00:28:19.140 --> 00:28:21.902
So I will investigate the memory
consumption of my application.

00:28:21.902 --> 00:28:23.610
And then I'll use
