00:31:12.030 --> 00:31:14.340
So I have created a
vertical pod autoscaler.

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:19.590
And the interesting bits
of these configurations

00:31:19.590 --> 00:31:22.700
are, first of all,
matching by label.

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:25.800
So vertical pod autoscaler is
different than horizontal pod

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:27.440
autoscaler.

00:31:27.440 --> 00:31:29.950
You don't point VPA at
specific controllers,

00:31:29.950 --> 00:31:31.641
such as deployment
or replica set.

00:31:31.641 --> 00:31:33.390
Instead, it is a little
bit more flexible.

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:37.410
And you can select
specific pods that you

00:31:37.410 --> 00:31:39.270
want the VPA to generate
a recommendation

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:41.430
for through a label query.

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:43.980
In this case, it's app worker.

00:31:43.980 --> 00:31:47.070
Now, the next important
piece of configuration

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:49.750
here is the update policy.

00:31:49.750 --> 00:31:52.280
So in this case, I set
the update modes to off

00:31:52.280 --> 00:31:54.210
because all I want
to know is I want

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:56.090
to see VPAs recommendations.

00:31:56.090 --> 00:31:59.220
And all I'm hoping
that this will give me

00:31:59.220 --> 00:32:01.950
some more clues about what's
going on in the system.

00:32:01.950 --> 00:32:05.150
As a reminder, we can also
set up the update modes

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:11.720
to auto, in which case the
vertical pod autoscaler would

00:32:11.720 --> 00:32:14.250
do the rolling
update of all my pods

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:17.520
that are matching the
label query and recreating

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:22.320
them, basically killing them and
letting them be scheduled again

00:32:22.320 --> 00:32:25.259
with the new resource requests.

00:32:25.259 --> 00:32:27.300
The reason is that currently
in Kubernetes, there

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:29.730
is no functionality,
there's no capability

00:32:29.730 --> 00:32:32.330
of adjusting the
resource requests

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:34.160
and limits for running pods.

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:35.910
So you basically have
to kill them and let

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:37.701
them be re-scheduled
again by the scheduler

00:32:37.701 --> 00:32:40.200
with the new requests.

00:32:40.200 --> 00:32:43.320
All right, so in the
meantime, let's see

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:51.360
if our VPA has generated any
good recommendations for us.

00:32:56.730 --> 00:32:58.620
All right, it did, actually.

00:32:58.620 --> 00:33:03.560
So there is this, in VPA
resource, which is a customer

00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:06.870
service definition, we've got
this recommendation section

00:33:06.870 --> 00:33:10.670
where we see that
our VPA says, hmm,

00:33:10.670 --> 00:33:12.930
these worker tasks should
be probably running

00:33:12.930 --> 00:33:17.560
at a target of 3.5 gigs
of RAM memory request.

00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:20.940
So this is totally not
something that my infrastructure

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:21.630
is prepared for.

00:33:21.630 --> 00:33:23.730
My nodes were 2.6 gigs.

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:26.140
So I'm going to
be very naive now.

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:28.620
I don't want to deal
with infrastructure.

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:31.450
I just want to actuate vertical
pod autoscaler's decision.

00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:34.695
So what I'm going to do is
I'm going to edit this VPA.

00:33:38.170 --> 00:33:43.090
And I'm going to change
only one parameter, which

00:33:43.090 --> 00:33:43.890
is the update mode.

00:33:47.730 --> 00:33:51.480
So I'm changing to auto.

00:33:51.480 --> 00:33:52.560
Boom.

00:33:52.560 --> 00:33:54.250
And let's see what will happen.

00:33:54.250 --> 00:33:56.720
In the meantime, I'm
heading over to Jerzy.

00:33:56.720 --> 00:33:58.320
JERZY FORYCIARZ: All right.

00:33:58.320 --> 00:34:00.320
So let's go back to slides.

00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:03.760
So now we're going to
let the magic happen.

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:08.246
In the meantime, let's talk
about the proper magic.

00:34:08.246 --> 00:34:10.620
So in this section, we're
going to talk about the cluster

00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:14.090
autoscaling.

00:34:14.090 --> 00:34:18.989
[? And you'll see ?] you
may expect what would happen

00:34:18.989 --> 00:34:20.409
in the normal situation.

00:34:20.409 --> 00:34:23.810
We would have the
automation that we set up,

00:34:23.810 --> 00:34:31.420
the vertical pod autoscaler
to attempt killing the pods.

00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:34.360
And then we would
attempt to recreate them

00:34:34.360 --> 00:34:36.360
with a different pod request.

00:34:36.360 --> 00:34:38.017
The pod request
would have been--

00:34:38.017 --> 00:34:40.350
the memory request would have
been something around 3.2,

00:34:40.350 --> 00:34:42.880
if I remember.

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:45.159
So that would be pending.

00:34:45.159 --> 00:34:50.949
So we would literally
kill our set up forever,

00:34:50.949 --> 00:34:56.050
until somebody on the operator
site, on your DevOps side,

00:34:56.050 --> 00:34:57.970
would have realized,
well, something is wrong.

00:34:57.970 --> 00:35:00.280
We cannot-- these
are not scheduled.

00:35:00.280 --> 00:35:06.300
So luckily, this is
not how it's working.

00:35:06.300 --> 00:35:09.370
We told you you can forget
about the infrastructure

00:35:09.370 --> 00:35:11.900
because the infrastructure
is not forgetting about you.

00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:15.680
So this is exactly
what's happening here.

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:20.820
Cluster autoscaler works
through observing the pod

00:35:20.820 --> 00:35:26.470
requests and, based
on these requests,

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:28.660
making a decision or suggestion.

00:35:28.660 --> 00:35:30.350
So what you have
here is a scheduler.

00:35:30.350 --> 00:35:33.270
And the scheduler is,
in a normal situation,

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:37.210
is looking at the pod's requests
and it's placing a note.

00:35:37.210 --> 00:35:41.324
So if there is enough
resources on the node-- sorry,

00:35:41.324 --> 00:35:42.490
it's [? plaguing ?] the pod.

00:35:42.490 --> 00:35:48.580
If there is enough resources
on a node to deploy a pod,

00:35:48.580 --> 00:35:51.230
it's simply happening.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:55.810
But there may be a situation if
there is not enough resources

00:35:55.810 --> 00:36:00.680
and you necessarily need
to add one node, like this.

00:36:00.680 --> 00:36:03.480
Well, there is an
operation involved.

00:36:03.480 --> 00:36:05.320
What it this triggered by?

00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:07.860
This is triggered by the
pending pods, nothing else.

00:36:07.860 --> 00:36:09.960
No utilization of any kind.

00:36:09.960 --> 00:36:14.230
The reaction is to,
well, do I need more?

00:36:14.230 --> 00:36:17.110
In some cases, you
may be in a situation

00:36:17.110 --> 00:36:21.220
like this, which is even more
annoying because now you have

00:36:21.220 --> 00:36:24.130
too many nodes, it looks like.

00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:25.840
Too many nodes.

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:28.120
So in the ideal
case, you would have

00:36:28.120 --> 00:36:30.600
done some kind of a defrag.

00:36:30.600 --> 00:36:32.680
Realistically, move
one of the pods

00:36:32.680 --> 00:36:36.130
somewhere else so that
you can remove the node

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:40.630
and have a better utilization,
a better price of performance,

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:41.770
as you may say.

00:36:41.770 --> 00:36:48.460
So this is all done by
the cluster autoscaler.

00:36:48.460 --> 00:36:53.710
Cluster autoscaler is doing
literally two functions.

00:36:53.710 --> 00:36:57.180
And within each of
those, other three.

00:36:57.180 --> 00:37:00.430
So it watches the pending
pods, first of all.

00:37:00.430 --> 00:37:04.240
It's just seeing what the
scheduler would be seeing.

00:37:04.240 --> 00:37:06.070
And the pending
pod means there is

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:12.670
no space in the infrastructure
for the pod to be scheduled.

00:37:12.670 --> 00:37:14.620
And then it simulates
the scheduler

00:37:14.620 --> 00:37:20.290
by trying to look into what
would have happened if I added

00:37:20.290 --> 00:37:22.900
one or two or three nodes.

00:37:22.900 --> 00:37:25.660
Would the deployment
actually land?

00:37:25.660 --> 00:37:29.740
And would all of the
pods be scheduled?

00:37:29.740 --> 00:37:34.010
If that's the case, then yes.

00:37:34.010 --> 00:37:36.110
Cluster autoscaler
triggers adding nodes.

00:37:36.110 --> 00:37:40.200
This is done by the part
of the infrastructure.

00:37:40.200 --> 00:37:43.670
But the decision counts
from the cluster autoscaler.

00:37:43.670 --> 00:37:47.620
Now, in the same time, it
also watches the utilization.

00:37:47.620 --> 00:37:53.210
But it only watches it for the
construction of the cluster.

00:37:53.210 --> 00:37:59.330
So if any node is running
below some set utilization,

00:37:59.330 --> 00:38:01.800
right now I believe
we have it set to 50%,

00:38:01.800 --> 00:38:05.139
this is a trigger to look
around for the-- this

00:38:05.139 --> 00:38:06.680
is a trigger for a
cluster autoscaler

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:10.490
to look around and make
some checks, right?

00:38:10.490 --> 00:38:13.350
What would have happened
if we deleted that machine?

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:15.230
Would all pods be rescheduled?

00:38:15.230 --> 00:38:17.340
Or are they in the system
nodes running there

00:38:17.340 --> 00:38:21.900
and that would be
a big disruption?

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:25.240
How would that play with
the pod disruption budget?

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:29.850
If it's possible, the decision
comes to train and delete

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:33.200
the node, and then
the scheduler is

00:38:33.200 --> 00:38:37.460
rescheduling the pods that were
running there somewhere else.

00:38:37.460 --> 00:38:42.300
And through this, we have
a better package system.

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:45.980
So that was a very
intro work through.

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:50.670
But the real cluster autoscaler
looks something like that

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:54.950
because in a cluster, you don't
just have one type of nodes.

00:38:54.950 --> 00:38:56.670
Well, you could.

00:38:56.670 --> 00:39:01.620
But in most cases, you are
deploying more than one type

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:05.700
of the workload, more
than one size of the pods.

00:39:05.700 --> 00:39:09.810
And it's very hard,
unless you manage

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:15.840
to deploy super big nodes that
can accommodate everything

00:39:15.840 --> 00:39:18.540
and you don't care for the
empty space surrounding on them,

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:21.420
it's really super
hard to optimize it

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:23.270
with just one type of nodes.

00:39:23.270 --> 00:39:26.010
That's why the good
practice that we

00:39:26.010 --> 00:39:29.970
were suggesting, and was
there for at least last year,

00:39:29.970 --> 00:39:33.180
maybe a year and a half,
was to use in node pools.

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:36.570
Node pools are groups
of identical nodes

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:38.730
within the cluster.

00:39:38.730 --> 00:39:40.520
And example like
this one is where

00:39:40.520 --> 00:39:44.890
you have some small nodes,
some medium, and some big.

00:39:44.890 --> 00:39:49.780
And through this, the
cluster autoscaler

00:39:49.780 --> 00:39:51.210
does a little bit different job.

00:39:54.450 --> 00:39:58.360
It is monitoring the state
of the entire cluster,

00:39:58.360 --> 00:39:59.680
but it's also--

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:04.140
whenever there is
a pending pod, it's

00:40:04.140 --> 00:40:08.060
actually simulating
the action of scheduler

00:40:08.060 --> 00:40:09.940
across all of the node pools.

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:12.570
So what would that
have been if we

00:40:12.570 --> 00:40:16.510
enlarge one of this, or the
other one, or the other one?

00:40:16.510 --> 00:40:18.930
Well, this is a very
simple example when

00:40:18.930 --> 00:40:20.490
you look at this single pod.

00:40:20.490 --> 00:40:22.510
But the pods are
coming in all the time,

00:40:22.510 --> 00:40:26.100
and they are of different sizes.

00:40:26.100 --> 00:40:29.760
So realistically, there is lots
of interesting combinatorics

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:30.780
coming in.

00:40:30.780 --> 00:40:35.760
But thanks to this, you
end up getting very well

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:37.120
and tight packaged cluster.

00:40:39.750 --> 00:40:44.690
So when the recommendation
is placed then

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:50.910
the new node is added in
the node pool of a choosing

00:40:50.910 --> 00:40:57.290
by the cluster autoscaler, and
then the pod gets deployed.

00:40:57.290 --> 00:41:00.810
Now, there may be a
situation similar to what

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:06.090
we're setting our demo for,
that despite the fact that we

00:41:06.090 --> 00:41:10.320
already have three types
of node pools, three

00:41:10.320 --> 00:41:14.810
sizes of node pools, we now
arrive with the pod that

00:41:14.810 --> 00:41:17.580
wouldn't fit to any of those.

00:41:17.580 --> 00:41:22.510
And before November,
late November

00:41:22.510 --> 00:41:25.720
last year, that would
be the moment where

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:28.180
your system would get stuck.

00:41:28.180 --> 00:41:29.920
And it would have
to wait for someone

00:41:29.920 --> 00:41:31.940
to notice there is a problem.

00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:35.170
Luckily, and this
feature is now in alpha

00:41:35.170 --> 00:41:38.800
and it's coming to
beta later this summer,

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:40.960
we have node
autoprovisioning, which

00:41:40.960 --> 00:41:44.460
is like a consultant to
the cluster autoscaler.

00:41:44.460 --> 00:41:46.180
So cluster autoscaler
is not only

00:41:46.180 --> 00:41:49.210
running the simulation
looking at all three

00:41:49.210 --> 00:41:50.390
of these node pools.

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:54.080
But in fact, it's consulting
node autoprovisioning saying,

00:41:54.080 --> 00:41:56.720
what if there was another one?

00:41:56.720 --> 00:41:59.740
And if there was, what
would be the size?

00:41:59.740 --> 00:42:01.900
And then at the end,
it decides there

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:04.220
is a new node pool needed.

00:42:04.220 --> 00:42:05.830
It creates one.

00:42:05.830 --> 00:42:08.650
And then the pod gets deployed.

00:42:08.650 --> 00:42:12.750
Now throughout this
talk, we are telling you

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:15.880
you can forget about
node provisioning.

00:42:15.880 --> 00:42:18.130
So we're just
explaining it to you

00:42:18.130 --> 00:42:20.140
so that you see
how simple it is.

00:42:20.140 --> 00:42:22.060
But we leave you
very little control,

00:42:22.060 --> 00:42:25.990
just like you are switching
a cluster autoscaler simply

00:42:25.990 --> 00:42:26.890
on and off.

00:42:26.890 --> 00:42:28.840
And you also get as
a budget the minimum

00:42:28.840 --> 00:42:32.170
and the maximum of
RAM of CPU, you just

00:42:32.170 --> 00:42:34.390
enable autoprovisioning.

00:42:34.390 --> 00:42:36.440
You don't need to
worry about this layer.

00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:39.000
This layer just does the
job for your higher layer.

00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:40.920
The higher layer is
where your workload is,

00:42:40.920 --> 00:42:42.640
so you better know
what's happening.

00:42:42.640 --> 00:42:45.670
This one is just
adjusting to get you

00:42:45.670 --> 00:42:48.526
the best cost for performance.

00:42:48.526 --> 00:42:50.440
OK, so that's roughly it.

00:42:50.440 --> 00:42:53.890
So let me just walk you
through what just happened.

00:42:53.890 --> 00:42:59.410
And then we're going to have
a look into the demo again.

00:42:59.410 --> 00:43:02.440
So we had a horizontal
pod autoscaler

00:43:02.440 --> 00:43:08.320
set to look at the
Pub/Sub queue as parameter

00:43:08.320 --> 00:43:12.700
and autoscale so that
for every 25 tasks,

00:43:12.700 --> 00:43:15.040
there was at least
one worker deployed.

00:43:15.040 --> 00:43:20.470
We had vertical pod
autoscaler to watch,

00:43:20.470 --> 00:43:22.990
after we enabled it
into the auto mode, now

00:43:22.990 --> 00:43:26.740
it's watching if there
is everything right

00:43:26.740 --> 00:43:30.010
with the requests of resources.

00:43:30.010 --> 00:43:32.980
And if not, we actually
set it to auto.

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:37.930
And as a result, whenever
the request is wrong,

00:43:37.930 --> 00:43:39.850
it will get adjusted.

00:43:39.850 --> 00:43:44.230
And then on the
infrastructure layer,

00:43:44.230 --> 00:43:49.510
the cluster autoscaler is
observing the unscheduled pods.

00:43:49.510 --> 00:43:51.820
And it's actually
consulting with the VPA

00:43:51.820 --> 00:43:55.780
so that this process
doesn't happen prematurely

00:43:55.780 --> 00:43:56.950
in an awkward way.

00:43:56.950 --> 00:44:00.460
We don't want all of the
pods to be killed first

00:44:00.460 --> 00:44:02.120
and then be pending later.

00:44:02.120 --> 00:44:07.410
So in fact, there is a loopback,
which there is a translation of

00:44:07.410 --> 00:44:09.550
are these resources
already prepared?

00:44:09.550 --> 00:44:12.570
And only if they are,
the update is happening.

00:44:12.570 --> 00:44:16.240
Most of the real life cases
don't happen that drastically.

00:44:16.240 --> 00:44:18.880
They happen every
once in a while.

00:44:18.880 --> 00:44:20.350
And then the node
autoprovisioning

00:44:20.350 --> 00:44:22.760
is a consultant to the
cluster autoscaler.

00:44:22.760 --> 00:44:25.040
It's making it more optimal.

00:44:25.040 --> 00:44:29.000
So as you can see, you
have four autoscaler.

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:34.580
But in fact, you
have one system that

00:44:34.580 --> 00:44:40.720
is autoscaling all of it
for you by having all four

00:44:40.720 --> 00:44:42.770
layers talking in unison.

00:44:42.770 --> 00:44:44.220
So that's what we have.

00:44:44.220 --> 00:44:45.492
And now are we ready?

00:44:45.492 --> 00:44:46.450
IGNACY KOWALCZYK: Yeah.

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:47.574
JERZY FORYCIARZ: All right.

00:44:47.574 --> 00:44:50.019
IGNACY KOWALCZYK:
Well, we'll see.

00:44:50.019 --> 00:44:51.060
All right, thanks, Jerzy.

00:44:51.060 --> 00:44:53.090
So this is the final
step of our demo.

00:44:53.090 --> 00:44:55.940
So we're going to
see what happens.

00:44:55.940 --> 00:44:58.309
So remember, I was playing
only with pod autoscaler--

00:44:58.309 --> 00:45:00.850
sorry, horizontal pod autoscaler
and vertical pod autoscaler.

00:45:00.850 --> 00:45:03.850
And thankfully, I got my cluster
set up with this smart node

00:45:03.850 --> 00:45:05.680
autoprovisioning and
cluster autoscaler

00:45:05.680 --> 00:45:09.920
enabled so that I did not
care about my nodes at all.

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:12.140
So now let's see what happened.

00:45:12.140 --> 00:45:14.710
So I'm going back
to the console.

00:45:14.710 --> 00:45:17.560
And I have my little script
that brings the pods,

00:45:17.560 --> 00:45:19.380
but also brings the--

00:45:19.380 --> 00:45:23.480
let me show it to
you, actually--

00:45:23.480 --> 00:45:28.850
but also brings the memory
requests of those pods.

00:45:28.850 --> 00:45:32.570
So remember, when I set up
the podspec for my deployment,

00:45:32.570 --> 00:45:34.909
the memory units were empty.

00:45:34.909 --> 00:45:36.200
So let's see what they are now.

00:45:41.012 --> 00:45:42.720
So again, you have
all these evicted pods

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:46.380
that we had before, but we
also have four running pods.

00:45:46.380 --> 00:45:50.040
And all these pods have
actually the memory request

00:45:50.040 --> 00:45:53.026
set to 3.5 five gigs, which
was the recommendation

00:45:53.026 --> 00:45:54.150
of vertical pod autoscaler.

00:45:54.150 --> 00:45:55.150
So woohoo, this works.

00:45:55.150 --> 00:45:56.191
And they are running now.

00:45:56.191 --> 00:45:59.160
Now how is it possible,
given that I had

00:45:59.160 --> 00:46:01.894
five nodes with 2.7 gigs each?

00:46:01.894 --> 00:46:03.310
So let's take a
look at the nodes.

00:46:03.310 --> 00:46:04.690
I'm using my old script--

00:46:04.690 --> 00:46:06.190
JERZY FORYCIARZ:
Before we go there,

00:46:06.190 --> 00:46:08.800
the historical data that
we are showing there

00:46:08.800 --> 00:46:10.500
is like all of the failed nodes.

00:46:10.500 --> 00:46:12.390
But you look at the
four that are running.

00:46:12.390 --> 00:46:15.630
We requested four to be running,
and there are four running.

00:46:15.630 --> 00:46:21.180
IGNACY KOWALCZYK: So indeed,
we have our original v5 nodes

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:22.740
in the default node pool.

00:46:22.740 --> 00:46:27.120
But we also have a new
n1-standard-2 VMs in a new node

00:46:27.120 --> 00:46:30.030
pool, created automatically by
all the node autoprovisioner.

00:46:30.030 --> 00:46:32.660
And these guys are running
5 gigs of RAM each.

00:46:32.660 --> 00:46:36.745
And thanks to that, our
worker task gets scheduled.

00:46:36.745 --> 00:46:38.640
So it all works.

00:46:38.640 --> 00:46:40.850
Let me take a look
at the final--

00:46:40.850 --> 00:46:44.220
at the Stackdriver
chart, refresh it,

00:46:44.220 --> 00:46:47.580
and let's see if
it looks better.

00:46:47.580 --> 00:46:48.090
Indeed.

00:46:48.090 --> 00:46:53.190
We see that the workers
grew to bigger memory usage

00:46:53.190 --> 00:46:54.090
than before.

00:46:54.090 --> 00:46:56.340
They were allowed to and
they are stabilizing.

00:46:56.340 --> 00:46:58.700
They are running at this level.

00:46:58.700 --> 00:47:00.440
So wow.

00:47:00.440 --> 00:47:01.420
[APPLAUSE]

00:47:01.420 --> 00:47:02.545
JERZY FORYCIARZ: All right.

00:47:07.300 --> 00:47:12.620
OK, so let's go back to slides.

00:47:12.620 --> 00:47:14.900
This was great.

00:47:14.900 --> 00:47:17.900
So we hope you see
that you can really

00:47:17.900 --> 00:47:20.900
forget about node provisioning.

00:47:20.900 --> 00:47:25.550
Well, in fact, you can set it
up and almost observe that it

00:47:25.550 --> 00:47:28.360
occasionally goes to zero or
almost to zero because there

00:47:28.360 --> 00:47:32.390
are still some system-level
polls that needs to be running

00:47:32.390 --> 00:47:33.170
and only--

00:47:33.170 --> 00:47:35.840
or are that when you need it.

00:47:35.840 --> 00:47:38.872
So you may wonder, is that all?

00:47:38.872 --> 00:47:41.960
It should be all because we
have one more minute and then

00:47:41.960 --> 00:47:44.140
questions.

00:47:44.140 --> 00:47:47.910
But, well, one more thing.

00:47:47.910 --> 00:47:52.520
Well, wouldn't it be
great if that also worked

00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:55.220
with the graphics accelerators?

00:47:55.220 --> 00:47:56.860
Well, it does.

00:47:56.860 --> 00:47:59.720
With the new release
that's coming to beta soon,

00:47:59.720 --> 00:48:03.390
node autoprovisioning will
be capable of scheduling

00:48:03.390 --> 00:48:05.570
your nodes with the
graphics accelerators.

00:48:05.570 --> 00:48:09.290
So now imagine this
very precious resource

00:48:09.290 --> 00:48:15.360
that is super expensive gets
super automatically provisioned

00:48:15.360 --> 00:48:16.950
only when you need it.

00:48:16.950 --> 00:48:21.910
So you deploy your
pod, your pod request

00:48:21.910 --> 00:48:23.280
just has one extra line.

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:27.510
And it says what type
of graphics card I want.

00:48:27.510 --> 00:48:31.050
And to the four steps
that we were describing--

00:48:31.050 --> 00:48:33.410
in fact, three, because
the horizontal pod

00:48:33.410 --> 00:48:36.780
autoscaler doesn't need to be
part of it, you'll just get it.

00:48:36.780 --> 00:48:40.740
And then whenever it's not
needed, it will be removed.

00:48:40.740 --> 00:48:46.260
But in fact, a while ago you
saw there were four new nodes.

00:48:46.260 --> 00:48:49.102
And there were also
still all the old nodes.

00:48:49.102 --> 00:48:50.310
They're going to get removed.

00:48:50.310 --> 00:48:53.970
There is a cycle loop that's
running every 10 minutes, so

00:48:53.970 --> 00:48:55.034
in a while--

00:48:55.034 --> 00:48:56.700
we've been speaking
for very short time.

00:48:56.700 --> 00:48:58.075
In a while, they
will get removed

00:48:58.075 --> 00:48:59.950
because this is how it works.

00:48:59.950 --> 00:49:01.047
All right, so--

00:49:01.047 --> 00:49:03.630
IGNACY KOWALCZYK: And just, you
can also imagine the direction

00:49:03.630 --> 00:49:05.700
where it's going is
that it doesn't have

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:07.830
to be only size of the
nodes and only GPUs,

00:49:07.830 --> 00:49:11.230
but whatever infrastructure
requirements of your workload.

00:49:11.230 --> 00:49:14.190
So basically the idea is
you specify declaratively

00:49:14.190 --> 00:49:17.520
the requests, the requirements
from the infrastructure

00:49:17.520 --> 00:49:20.355
in your podspecs or
in your controllers.

00:49:20.355 --> 00:49:20.855
That's it.

00:49:20.855 --> 00:49:22.550
You don't have to care
about the infrastructure.

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:24.716
The right infrastructure
will be provisioned for you

00:49:24.716 --> 00:49:25.860
by node autoprovisioning.

00:49:25.860 --> 00:49:26.190
JERZY FORYCIARZ: All right.

00:49:26.190 --> 00:49:27.034
So that's all.

00:49:27.034 --> 00:49:28.950
Now we have a couple of
minutes for questions.

00:49:28.950 --> 00:49:31.470
And then we'll be outside
to take more questions.

